<?xml version='1.0' ?>
<rrx namespace="AutomaticDocumentFingerprinting" v="9.1.9.10">
  <com ref="Datacap.Libraries.AutomaticDocumentFingerprinting.Actions"  qi=": Identifies pages and assigns types and fingerprints through fingerprint matching.">

    <h>
      When ingesting and processing documents in a Datacap application, typically documents and pages are classified to identify the data within
      the document or to control how the page should processed.  Fingerprints are a classification mechanism that matches images
      based on the geometry of the image.  It is typical for a Datacap application to use fingerprints to classify pages
      within the workflow.  It is allowed for other types of classification techniques to be used in addition to fingerprints.
      The rules within an application are flexible to allow exclusive use of fingerprints for classification or mixing fingerprinting
      with other types of classification, including techniques that may be custom for an application.<br/><br/>

      Classification can be configured uniquely for any Datacap application.  With that said, there are some typical types of classification
      that are performed and are briefly described here.  The intent is to provide basic understanding of fingerprints, how they can be used
      as a starting point that can be used in an application which are then changed or expanded as required by a specific application's
      use cases.<br/><br/>

      One approach to classification is by using separator sheets.  These are typically sheets with a barcode and are used to separate
      different pages that are scanned.  The presence of a new separator sheet, denotes the start of a new document.  Fingerprints
      can classify documents in addition to or instead of separator sheets.  To fingerprint a page, the page needs to be analyzed,
      using the AnalyzeImage action, to create a CCO file that is a geometric representation of the page.  This representation will then
      be matched using the FindFingerprint action against known formats. When match, the page type is then determined. Additionally
      a fingerprint template is assigned to the page, the fingerprint template defines zones / fields on the page that contain
      the data that will be extracted or verified by an operator.  While classification can be performed without using fields,
      it is a typical scenario where fields are also used.<br/><br/>

      Using the APT application as an example, a page may be identified as a "MainPage" which is the first page of
      an invoice.  This causes different processing for that type of page, opposed to other types such as separator
      pages which may be ignored or deleted.  An application may process invoices from 1000s of different vendors.
      One invoice may be from "ABC Plumbing" and another may be from "XYZ Car Rental".  Because they are provided by different
      vendors, each will have their own format and layout to the invoice that was received.  However, there are some common properties
      of an invoice that the application needs to process such as date, vendor name, address, tax, total and line items for items
      covered by the invoice.  While each page is identified as a "MainPage", the matched fingerprint template for each page will
      be unique.  For example, ABC Plumbing may have the date in the top left corner of the page while XYZ Car Rental puts the
      date at the bottom of the page.  The matched template contains the location of the desired data so the application can
      correctly obtain the data from the invoice.  APT has additional abilities where it can ingest invoices that have not
      been previously processed and dynamically determine the locations of these values and then save a fingerprint so the
      locations are known the next time an invoice is received from the same vendor.<br/><br/>

      That is one example of how fingerprints can assign page types and field zone templates for pages.  Datacap has the ability
      to run unique rules for each object type within an application.  Perhaps pages defined as invoices need one set of operations
      and documents identified as bank statements need a different set of processing steps.  The page types determined by fingerprinting
      will then control the pages as the continue through the workflow.<br/><br/>

      Prior to fingerprinting, images are typically pre-processed to ensure the greatest accuracy when processed.  For example,
      images are typically straightened, de-speckled and have blobs and lines removed prior to fingerprinting.  This will help
      detection by applying a uniform look to pages prior to identification.  It is quite possible that it may not be desired to
      show these processed forms to the user, or more likely, it is desired to save the original documents as they came in and
      not the versions that have been adjusted for fingerprinting or recognition.  The Datacap actions always save the original
      version of the image prior to performing image adjustments.  This original version of the image can always be used or stored
      as required in later steps of the workflow.<br/><br/>

      <b>Local Fingerprints</b><br/><br/>

      By default fingerprints are stored and used "locally" on the machine that is running rules and are held within
      the client or process.  For example, fingerprints are typically used in a "Page Identification" task.
      When this process is performed, the AutomaticDocumentFingerprint actions will load the fingerprints
      defined for the application, then perform fingerprint matching and creation.  It is all local on the
      current machine.  This process occurs for each batch that is run.  The benefit of using fingerprints
      locally is that no additional setup is required beyond adding the required fingerprint actions to a
      Datacap application.  A draw back of local fingerprints are that the fingerprints will be loaded for
      each batch.  If there are a large number of fingerprints, this could be several minutes. It also will\
      consume memory within the process that is running the batch.  For better performance, the
      fingerprint service is the recommended mechanism for using fingerprints.<br/><br/>

      <b>Fingerprint Service</b><br/><br/>

      The Datacap Fingerprint Service, in combination with actions from this action library, supports the ability to cache and
      use the fingerprints from one or multiple Datacap applications simultaneously. The Fingerprint Service overcomes
      the time-consuming process of loading all the fingerprints of an application each time a batch is run.
      The solution involves reading all the fingerprints into the system memory cache of the Fingerprint Server when the
      first fingerprint match is requested. As a further optimization of this process, only the identification portion
      of the fingerprint is loaded into memory. The fingerprints remain in system memory for the life of the Fingerprint
      Service process. The result is the elimination of the fingerprint load time for all but the first batch that
      is processed by an application. Large-scale implementations, where large numbers of fingerprints
      (typically over 1000 fingerprints) are required for document identification, use of the Fingerprint Service is recommended.<br/><br/>

      Other than identifying the location of the fingerprint service with the SetFingerprintWebServiceURL, the actions
      within this action library will transparently use the fingerprint service vs the local service.  The important step is to
      first call the <b>SetFingerprintWebServiceURL</b> action to specify the location of the service, then subsequent actions,
      in the same profile, will automatically use the service instead of using local fingerprinting operations.<br/><br/>

      The fingerprint service requires IIS and can be configured on a machine which can be shared
      by multiple applications.  Refer to the fingerprint service configuration documentation
      "Installing and configuring the Datacap Fingerprint Service" for more information.<br/><br/>

      The first time that a match is requested by an application, the Fingerprint Service loads the fingerprints (all *cco files)
      for that application. When more than one application is set up to use a single Fingerprint Service,
      the first time each application requests fingerprint matching, the fingerprints for that application are loaded into memory.
      The greater the number of Fingerprints to read into memory, the longer the service takes to begin fingerprint processing;
      which can be from a few seconds to many minutes for large fingerprint deployments. Subsequent requests for matching
      work with the fingerprints in the cache in memory. When a matching fingerprint is not found and the
      application creates a new fingerprint, that new fingerprint is loaded into the cache.<br/><br/>

      The Datacap Fingerprint Service is a web service that is based on Microsoft Internet Information Services (IIS).
      The service can service multiple client requests simultaneously. The service can service multiple client requests simultaneously.
      The Datacap Fingerprint Service cache is shared by all rulerunners or clients running the application.
      Therefore, the single load of fingerprints into the cache for an application is all that is required even when multiple threads are running.<br/><br/>
      
      If the fingerprint service is enabled and an action that uses the fingerprint service is called, such as FindFingerprint, 
      and if the fingerprints are not yet loaded, or if the load is in progress due to it being initiated by another batch,
      by default the action will wait up to 20 minutes for the fingerprint load to complete.  The default time is long enough for most installations.
      Should more time be required due to a significantly large amount of fingerprints, set the DCO variable <b>FingerprintServiceWait_Seconds</b> to
      the maximum number of seconds that the action should wait for the fingerprints to load on the service.  This variable would be set on the
      same DCO node where FindFingerprint is called.  In other words, if one batch is loading fingerprints and another batch calls FindFingerprint,
      it will wait up to the 20 minute default time for the load to finish. If the load is not finished, the FindFingerprint action will not perform 
      a fingerprint match and will return false.  Increasing the timeout will avoid this problem during long fingerprint load times at the service.<br/><br/>
      
      Once the fingerprints are loaded by the services, they
      will remain loaded for subsequent calls to FindFingerprint and will not need to be reloaded.  If the service is recycled, then on the next
      call to the service, the fingerprints will be reloaded.<br/><br/>

        <b>XML Fingerprints</b><br/><br/>

        XML fingerprints are preferred over traditional/legacy fingerprints.  Fingerprints consist of two types of metadata. The first type is a
        CCO file that contains the fingerprint geometry information used when matching fingerprints. The second type of metadata are
        the zone locations on the fingerprint. Each zone identifies the location of text that is captured by an application.
        For example, typical areas of an invoice page would include a date, invoice number, customer name, etc. The zone location
        identifies each of these and the coordinates where the text exists on a page.<br/><br/>

        The key difference between these two types of fingerprints is how the zone information is stored.
        Traditional fingerprints store the zone information within the Setup DCO of an application.  The XML fingerprints
        store the zone information externally as associated files in the fingerprint directory.<br/><br/>

        The advantage of XML fingerprints becomes apparent as the number of application fingerprints increases.
        When the application's Setup DCO contains a large number of fingerprints, it can slow the processing
        of an application, even when not performing fingerprint operations.  The issue does not occur when using XML fingerprints.<br/><br/>

        If 'Enable FPXML' setting is checked in the application settings 'Main' tab within the Taskmaster Application Manager,
        when zoning fingerprints in Datacap Studio, the application will place field zone information into a fingerprint XML file instead of placing the zone
        information into the applications setup DCO.  One XML file is created per fingerprint.        
        If the application is using fingerprint XML files, then use the action XMLReadZones to load the field zone coordinates.<br/><br/>

        <b>Additional Information</b><br/><br/>

      It is recommended to review the best practices guide for recognition for tips on how to improve
      image quality prior to fingerprint matching and recognition.<br/><br/>

      Fingerprints rely on the creation of a CCO for the image that is associated
      with the DCO page.  The CCO is typically created by the AnalyzeImage
      action and/or with a recognition action. More information about the CCO can be found in the top level
      help of the "SharedRecognitionTools" action library.<br/><br/>

      The top-level help topic of the ApplicationObjects action library discusses the Datacap Object Hierarchy (DCO)
      including the different object levels and object variables. It is recommended that the DCO is well
      understood when writing a Datacap application.<br/><br/>

      <b>Smart Parameters</b><br/><br/>

      Parameters that use an "@" notation, such as "@X.xxxx", "@P.xxxx", "@STRING()", "@PILOT()", etc., are
      known as "smart parameters". The data passed as a parameter to an action will be evaluated at runtime.
      The complete smart parameter reference is available in the top-level help of the RuleRunnerLogic action library.<br/><br/>
    </h>


    <method name="CalculateOffset" access="public" qi="Sets the standard Offset value to be used when matching source pages to fingerprints.">
      <ap>None.</ap>
      <lvl>Page level only.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the action is not applied at the Page level or an error occurs. Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <h>
        Calculates the offset, in pixels, between the CCO for the current page and the fingerprint that
        it matches.  The fingerprint template must have already been set from a previous call to FindFingerprint,
        SetFingerprint or some other method to associate the correct template with the current page.<br/><br/>

        The result of the calculation is stored in the current page object in a variable called "Image_Offset"
        in the form "x,y", where the value x is the pixel difference on the x-axis and y is the
        pixel difference on the y-axis.<br/><br/>

        The confidence of the match is stored in the current DCO variable called "Confidence".<br/><br/>
        <e>
          FindFingerprint("False","")<br/>
          <b>CalculateOffset()</b><br/><br/>

          This example will compare the CCO for the current image with the matched fingerprint and store the difference
          in the variable "Image_Offset".  For example, "-24,-16"<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
    </method>

      <method name="AddAndSetFingerprint" access="public" qi="Creates a fingerprint for the current source page with a specified class type.">
          <p name="FingerprintClass" type="string" />
          <ap>
              <b>FingerprintClass:</b> The fingerprint class name to assign to the new fingerprint.  If this parameter is
              blank, then the global name '˂New˃' will be assigned.  Smart parameters are supported.<br/><br/>
          </ap>
          <h>
              Unconditionally adds the current fingerprint for the current source page to the fingerprint
              directory for future fingerprint matching and allows the host fingerprint class to be specified.<br/><br/>

              The current page must have an existing, black and white, image file (.tif) and its fingerprint processing file (.cco).
              These files are given a unique fingerprint template ID and added to the fingerprint database.
              The TIF image and the CCO file will be added to the fingerprint directory.<br/><br/>

              Upon successful completion of the action, the following variables will be created in the
              current page DCO:<br/><br/>

              "Confidence" are created with a value of "1".<br/>
              "TemplateID" are set with the new ID associated with the fingerprint.<br/>
              "Fingerprint Created" are set to "Yes".<br/><br/>

              The resulting fingerprint will consist of two files: the page's Image file (.tif) and its Processing file (.cco).<br/><br/>

              SetFingerprintDirectory must have been called before this action.  If using the fingerprint service, then
              SetFingerprintWebServiceURL must also have been previously called.<br/>
              <e>
                  SetFingerprintDirectory("@APPPATH(fingerprint)")<br/>
                  AnalyzeImage()<br/>
                  <b>AddAndSetFingerprint("Invoice")</b><br/><br/>

                  This example configures the fingerprint directory then calls AnalyzeImage to create a fingerprint for the
                  current page based on the page's associated TIF image.  The fingerprint is then added to the
                  current collection of fingerprints.  Note that while SetFingerprintDirectory is shown in this example,
                  it is typically called on a batch level so it is set only once.
                  For more information, see SetFingerprintDirectory.<br/>
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>Page level only.</lvl>
          <ret>
              <b>False</b> if the rule with this action is not bound to a Page object of the Document Hierarchy;
              if the current page does not have an Image file; if a CCO fingerprint file has not been
              created or if the fingerprint's two files cannot be created in the fingerprint directory.
              Otherwise, <b>True.</b><br/>
          </ret>
          <see>
              SetFingerprintDir, SetFingerprintWebServiceURL<br/><br/>

              Refer to the top-level help for more information on using fingerprints and the fingerprint service.<br/><br/>
          </see>
      </method>



      <method name="AddFingerprint" access="public" qi="Creates a fingerprint for the current source page.">
      <ap>None.</ap>
      <h>
        Unconditionally adds the current fingerprint for the current source page to the fingerprint
        directory for future fingerprint matching.<br/><br/>

          The fingerprint will be associated with the global host fingerprint class ID.
          If the new fingerprint must be associated with a specific fingerprint class, then use the
          AddAndSetFingerprint action.

          The current page must have an existing Image file (.tif) and its fingerprint processing file (.cco).
          These files will be given a unique fingerprint template ID and added to the fingerprint database.
          The TIF image and the CCO file will be added to the fingerprint directory.<br/><br/>

        Upon successful completion of the action, the following variables will be created in the
        current page DCO:<br/><br/>

        "Confidence" will be created with a value of "1".<br/>
        "TemplateID" will be set with the new ID associated with the fingerprint.<br/>
        "Fingerprint Created" will be set to "Yes".<br/><br/>

        The resulting fingerprint will consist of two files: the page's Image file (.tif) and its Processing file (.cco).<br/><br/>

        SetFingerprintDirectory must have been called prior to this action.  If using the fingerprint service, then
        SetFingerprintWebServiceURL must also have been previously called.<br/>
        <e>
          SetFingerprintDirectory("@APPPATH(fingerprint)")<br/>
          AnalyzeImage()<br/>
          <b>AddFingerprint()</b><br/><br/>

          This example configures the fingerprint directory then calls AnalyzeImage to create a fingerprint for the
          current page based on the page's associated TIF image.  The fingerprint is then added to the
          current collection of fingerprints.  Note that while SetFingerprintDirectory is shown in this example,
          it is typically called on a batch level so it is set only once.  Refer to SetFingerprintDirectory
          for more information.<br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Page level only.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the rule with this action is not bound to a Page object of the Document Hierarchy;
        if the current page does not have an Image file; if a CCO fingerprint file has not been
        created or if the fingerprint's two files cannot be created in the fingerprint directory.
        Otherwise, <b>True.</b><br/>
      </ret>
      <see>
        SetFingerprintDir, SetFingerprintWebServiceURL<br/><br/>

        Refer to the top-level help for more information on using fingerprints and the fingerprint service.<br/><br/>
      </see>
    </method>




    <method name="DeleteFingerprint" access="public" qi="Deletes the current page's fingerprint.">
      <ap>None.</ap>
      <lvl>Page level only.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always returns <b>True.</b> Under certain conditions the action will be unable to delete the fingerprint
        but will still return True. For example if the action is not applied at the Page level or if the
        current page does not have an fingerprint template set.
        Please review the action log file if DeleteFingerprint does not perform as expected.<br/>
      </ret>
      <h>
        Removes a fingerprint from the application and fingerprint database.
        Deletes the image file (.tif) and fingerprint file (.cco) of the current page's fingerprint from the
        application's fingerprint directory, and its record from the Fingerprint database.
        The current page must have an associated fingerprint template from a previous
        action such as FindFingerprint or nothing will be deleted.<br/><br/>

        SetFingerprintDirectory action must be called prior to this action.<br/><br/>

        If the fingerprint service is in use, the fingerprint will not be unloaded. Instead
        it will simply not be loaded the next time the service loads fingerprints.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          SetFingerprintDirectory("C:\ParentDirectory\Application\fingerprint")<br/>
          <b>DeleteFingerprint()</b><br/><br/>

          This example will set the fingerprint directory and then delete the fingerprint template
          that is associated with this page.  It is assumed that FindFingerprint, SetFingerprint
          or similar action has been previously run to find the fingerprint that is associated with
          the page. Note that while SetFingerprintDirectory is shown in this example,
          it is typically called on a batch level so it is set only once.  Refer to SetFingerprintDirectory
          for more information.<br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <see>
        SetFingerprintDirectory, AddFingerprint<br/><br/>

        Refer to the top-level help for general information about this action library.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>

      <method name="CheckForStickyFingerprint" access="public" qi="Checks if a new fingerprint applies to a follow on page in the same batch.">
          <ap>None.</ap>
          <h>
              For new fingerprints this action checks to see if there another matching fingerprint
              within the same batch that has already been verified, where it can
              use it to obtain zone information.  An example is when a batch contains two similar
              invoices, which have never been processed by the system before.  Once the first
              invoice is zoned during verify, the same new fingerprint can be used
              on the second invoice.  This is only needed on new matching fingerprints within the same batch
              because a fingerprints zones are saved at export time and are available for subsequent batches.<br/><br/>
              
              This action has a requirement that the line items parent is called "Details".
              <e>
                  <b>CheckForStickyFingerprint()</b>
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>Page level.</lvl>
          <ret>
              Always <b>True.</b>
          </ret>
      </method>

      <method name="FindBlackFingerprint" access="public" qi="Attempts to match black forms to fingerprints in the fingerprints directory of an application.">
      <p name="AddFingerprintIfNotFound" type="string" />
      <p name="PageType" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>AddFingerprintIfNotFound:</b> If an existing fingerprint is not matched for the
        current page, a value of "True" will cause a new fingerprint to be created based
        on the current page.  The new fingerprint will match this page on future
        calls to this action.  The value "False" will cause the action to return false
        if the page does not match an existing fingerprint and a new fingerprint will not be created.<br/><br/>

        <b>PageType:</b> If parameter "AddFingerprintIfNotFound" is "True", then the provided
        page type is assigned to the new fingerprint.  The parameter is option.  If it is blank
        and a new fingerprint is created, then the type assigned to the new fingerprint
        will be the same as the current page.<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        This action is similar to FindFingerprint and attempts to match black forms to an existing fingerprint.
        A "black" form is a page that contains a dense amount of lines that have not been removed and can provide
        better matching on these kinds of pages than FindFingerprint. An example of a black form is a typical
        medical claim form which has been faxed making all of the lines black instead of a typical medical form
        that uses a drop out color that allows a scanner to remove a specific color, leaving just the text on the image.
        The action can optionally create a new fingerprint that matches the current page if none of the existing fingerprints
        match the current image.<br/><br/>

        A CCO file must be associated with the current page prior to calling this action.  The CCO file is typically
        created using the action AnalyzeImage to create a geometric representation of the image that is used
        to match with existing fingerprints.<br/><br/>

        It is typically recommended to use image enhancement features, such as deskew to first adjust the image prior
        to creating the fingerprint.  This will improve the matching quality of the fingerprints and
        will also improve subsequent recognition, if it will also be performed on the image.<br/><br/>

        A fingerprint match will occur if the current fingerprint matches an existing fingerprint
        with a confidence higher than the value configured with the action SetMinimumMatchingTolerance.
        If multiple fingerprints match with a tolerance greater than the configured tolerance,
        then the fingerprint that has the best match is returned as the matching fingerprint.
        If multiple fingerprints have a the highest confidence match of "1", then the first fingerprint
        that matches at the highest confidence will be considered the best match.<br/><br/>

        This action library has other actions that can be used to adjust how fingerprints are matched, such
        as: SetFilterClassName, SetFilterPageType, SetFingerprintWebServiceFailureThreshold, SetMaximumMatchingOffset,
        SetFingerprintDirectory which must be used prior to using this action.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          RotateImage()<br/>
          AnalyzeImage()<br/>
          RecognizePageOCR_A()<br/>
          SetFingerprintSearchArea("0.0","0.5")<br/>
          SetMinimumMatchingTolerance("0.7")<br/>
          SetFingerprintDirectory("@APPPATH(fingerprint)")<br/>
          <b>FindBlackFingerprint("True","Invoice_Page")</b><br/><br/>

          In this sequence, the FindBlackFingerprint action will only use the top 50% of the current page to
          search for a match.  However, it will accept a match with a confidence of 0.7 or higher.  If no match is found,
          the sequence will create a new fingerprint and store it in the location specified by the
          SetFingerprintDirectory action.  If the parameter has been set to False, a new fingerprint
          will not be created in case there is no match.<br/><br/>

          For illustration, this example shows a number of settings such as SetMinimumMatchingTolerance,
          SetFingerprintSearchArea and SetFingerprintDirectory on the page object that is calling FindFingerprint.
          These actions only need to be called once and they will remain in effect for the rest of the
          task profile. They could be called once at the batch level. If implemented exactly as shown,
          then these actions will be called over and over for each page of this type, which is
          not required and will use some amount of unnecessary processing time.
          If fingerprints are used again in a later task profile, any global settings
          such as the fingerprint directory, the fingerprint service URL, search area, etc.
          must again be configured before using subsequent fingerprint actions.<br/><br/>

          If the parameter is instead been set to False, no fingerprint will be created if a match is not found.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Page level only.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the action is not applied at the Page level or if the first parameter is <b>False</b> and
        a fingerprint match does not occur. Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        SetFingerprintDirectory<br/><br/>

        Refer to the top-level help topic for more information about fingerprints and the fingerprint service.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="FindFingerprint" access="public" qi="Attempts to match the current page to a fingerprint and creates a new fingerprint if a match does not occur.">
      <p name="AddFingerprintIfNotFound" type="string" />
      <p name="PageType" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>AddFingerprintIfNotFound:</b> If an existing fingerprint is not matched for the
        current page, a value of "True" will cause a new fingerprint to be created based
        on the current page.  The new fingerprint will match this page on future
        calls to this action.  The value "False" will cause the action to return false
        if the page does not match an existing fingerprint and a new fingerprint will not be created.<br/><br/>

        <b>PageType:</b> If parameter "AddFingerprintIfNotFound" is "True", then the provided
        page type is assigned to the new fingerprint.  The parameter is option.  If it is blank
        and a new fingerprint is created, then the type assigned to the new fingerprint
        will be the same as the current page.<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <lvl>Page level only.</lvl>

      <h>
        This action will use fingerprint web service if fingerprint web URL is set by SetFingerprintWebServiceURL and if the 
        service is available. If the fingerprint web service is not available, then it will default to local fingerprint service.
        <br/><br/>

        This action attempts to match the image associated with the current page to an existing fingerprint.
        The action can optionally create a new fingerprint that matches the current page if none of the existing fingerprints
        match the current image.<br/><br/>

        A CCO file must be associated with the current page prior to calling this action.  The CCO file is typically
        created using the action AnalyzeImage to create a geometric representation of the image that is used
        to match with existing fingerprints.<br/><br/>

        It is typically recommended to use image enhancement features, such as deskew and line removal to first adjust the image prior
        to creating the fingerprint.  This will improve the matching quality of the fingerprints and
        will also improve subsequent recognition, if it will also be performed on the image.<br/><br/>

        A fingerprint match will occur if the current fingerprint matches an existing fingerprint
        with a confidence higher than the value configured with the action SetMinimumMatchingTolerance.
        If multiple fingerprints match with a tolerance greater than the configured tolerance,
        then the fingerprint that has the best match is returned as the matching fingerprint.
        If multiple fingerprints have a the highest confidence match of "1", then the first fingerprint
        that matches at the highest confidence will be considered the best match.<br/><br/>

        This action library has other actions that can be used to adjust how fingerprints are matched, such
        as: SetFilterClassName, SetFilterPageType, SetFingerprintWebServiceFailureThreshold, SetMaximumMatchingOffset,
        as: SetFilterClassName, SetFilterPageType, SetFingerprintWebServiceFailureThreshold,
        SetFingerprintDirectory must be used prior to using this action.<br/><br/>

        If required by Datacap Support, additional logging can be enabled by setting the batch level
        variable "EnableCCOLogging" to "1". The logging will provide additional log details
        during fingerprint matching. This setting is only applicable to local fingerprints. It has no effect
        if the fingerprint service is in use.  Enable this logging only when required by Datacap Support.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          RotateImage()<br/>
          AnalyzeImage()<br/>
          RecognizePageOCR_A()<br/>
          SetFingerprintSearchArea("0.0","0.5")<br/>
          SetMinimumMatchingTolerance("0.7")<br/>
          SetFingerprintDirectory("@APPPATH(fingerprint)")<br/>
          <b>FindFingerprint("True","Invoice_Page")</b><br/><br/>

          In this sequence, the FindFingerprint action will only use the top 50% of the current page to
          search for a match.  However, it will accept a match with a confidence of 0.7 or higher.  If no match is found,
          the sequence will create a new fingerprint and store it in the location specified by the
          SetFingerprintDirectory action.  If the parameter has been set to False, a new fingerprint
          will not be created in case there is no match.<br/><br/>

          For illustration, this example shows a number of settings such as SetMinimumMatchingTolerance,
          SetFingerprintSearchARea and SetFingerprintDirectory on the page object that is calling FindFingerprint.
          These actions only need to be called once and they will remain in effect for the rest of the
          task profile. They could be called once at the batch level. If implemented exactly as shown,
          then these actions will be called over and over for each page of this type, which is
          not required and will use some amount of unnecessary processing time.<br/><br/>

          If the parameter is instead been set to False, no fingerprint will be created if a match is not found.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the action is not applied at the Page level, or if the parameter is False and a
        fingerprint match does not occur. Otherwise, True.  If a new Fingerprint cannot be added, the
        action still returns <b>True.</b><br/>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top-level help topic of this action library for more information about using
        fingerprints and the fingerprint service.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="IdentifyBlankPagesBySize" qi="Uses the Image file's size to determine if the file represents a 'blank' page.">
      <p name="MaximumSize" type="string" />
      <p name="PageType" type="string" />
      <p name="PageToEvaluate" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>MaximumSize:</b> Numeric value (0 to 2147483647) indicating the maximum size in bytes that qualifies a page as a blank page.<br/>
        <b>PageType:</b> If the page is determined to be blank, the specified page type will be assigned to the page. This parameter cannot be blank.<br/>
        <b>PageToEvaluate:</b> Numeric value (0, 1 or 2) to designate which pages in a multi-page dco are to be evaluated.
        If this value is blank or invalid, then the value "0" will be used.<br/>
        <br/>
        Values 0, 1 and 2 mean the following:<br/>
        0 : Every page under the current DCO object will be evaluated.<br/>
        1 : Only "odd" dco page objects will be evaluated within the DCO. The first child, then the third child, the 5th child, etc.<br/>
        2 : Only "even" dco page objects will be evaluated within the DCO. The second child, then the fourth child, the 6th child, etc.<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/>
      </ap>
      <lvl>Batch, Document, or Page levels.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the MaximumSize or PageType parameter is invalid, the rule with this action is bound to a Field object of the Document Hierarchy
        or if an error occurs.
        Returns <b>true</b> if one or more child pages are identified as a "blank" page because the file size is smaller than the specified size.<br/><br/>
      </ret>
      <h>
        Uses a page object's image file's size to determine if the file represents a 'blank' page.
        The action looks exclusively at the physical file size to determine if the image is blank.
        It does not look at the pixels of the image to determine if the page is blank.
        The compression of a file will affect the size of a file and the ability of this action to accurately
        detect blank pages.  A file using a compression such as fax or LZW compression, will likely be very small
        if there are a small number of black pixels on an image.  A file that is uncompressed, will be the same
        size on disk regardless of the number of black or white pixels on the image, making this action ineffective.<br/><br/>

        The intent of this action is to identify pages that are likely to be blank because they are using
        file compression and the physical file size is very small.
        Any page or child page with an image file smaller than the size parameter (in bytes) will be
        assigned the Page Type value provided as a parameter.<br/><br/>

        The PageToEvalute parameter will determine if all child pages will be evaluated or if only
        alternating pages within the DCO structure are evaluated.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>IdentifyBlankPagesBySize("1000","Blank_Page","")</b><br/><br/>

          This example will evaluate the current object and the child objects.  If called on
          a page object, then just the page object will be evaluated. If called on a document object
          then all of the document's pages will be evaluated.  If called at the batch level, then all
          pages in the batch will be evaluated.
        </e>
      </h>
      <see>
        Refer to the top-level help topic for general information about this action library.<br/>
        A similar action, IsBlankPage, in the SharedRecognitionTools action library counts the number of words
        in a page's CCO, and if the count is smaller than the provided size, it is considered to be blank.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>

      <method name="IsFingerprintClass" access="public" qi="Test that the fingerprint belongs to the specified class.">
          <p name="FingerprintDatabase" type="string" qi="The connection to the fingerprint database."/>
          <p name="ClassName" type="string" qi="Test for the fingerprint class name."/>
          <ap>
              Smart parameters are supported.<br/>
          </ap>
          <h>
              This action connects to the fingerprint database and verifies that the 
              specified fingerprint class name contains the 
              fingerprint ID loaded for the current page.<br/><br/>
              <e>
                  CheckDCOStatus("75")<br/>
                  <b>IsFingerPrintClass("@APPVAR(*/fingerprintconn:cs)", "Invoice")</b><br/>
                  DeleteFingerprint()<br/>
                  IncrementVariable("@X.DeletedNewFingerprint", "1")<br/><br/>

                  A parameter of "Invoice" checks to see if the current fingerprint exists in the class called "Invoice",
                  and if it does, the action returns true and follow on actions can perform further processing in the same function.
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>
              Page Level.
          </lvl>
          <ret>
              <b>False,</b> if there is an error or if the
              current page's fingerprint ID does not exist in the specified class.
          </ret>
      </method>      
      

    <method name="MergeCCOsByType" access="public" qi="Merges the Fingerprint files (.cco) associated with Page objects of the Document Hierarchy.">
      <p name="TypesToMerge" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>TypesToMerge:</b> Comma-separated string values indicating the Page Types of the Document Hierarchy objects to be merged.
        At least one page type must be provided for CCO files to be merged.<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported.<br/>
      </ap>
      <lvl>Document, Page or Field.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if a Fingerprint file (.cco) for one of the Page Types is not available or if an error occurs
        while merging. Otherwise, <b>True.</b>  If none of the pages match the specified page type,
        the action will still return <b>True.</b><br/>
      </ret>
      <h>
        Merges the Fingerprint files (.cco) associated with Page objects of the Document Hierarchy.<br/><br/>

        The CCO for each page under the document node are merged with the CCO for the first page
        within the document.  The final result will be that the CCO for the first page has been updated
        to contain the CCOs for each following page merged into the first page CCO.<br/><br/>

        Only the CCO for a page that has an associated TIF image can be merged.  If a page does
        not have a CCO or if the associated page image is not a TIF image, then it will not
        be merged.<br/><br/>

        If called on a page or field level, then the current DCO node must have a parent document
        object.  The CCOs for each page under the parent document DCO node will be merged
        together.  For example, if called on a page, the pages under the current document will have
        their CCO's merged together.  If the page does not have a parent document, such as the page type
        "Other" which has a batch as a parent, then this action will not merge the CCOs.<br/><br/>

        <b>Created DCO Variables:</b><br/><br/>

        This action creates several DCO variables in the first page node that
        has been merged with the rest of he pages.  The variables that contain the information about the merge.<br/><br/>

        <b>CCOBlock</b> : The DCO variable "CCOBlock" will be set with the name of the merged CCO file.<br/><br/>

        <b>id.CCO_n</b> : The name of this variable is comprised of the ID of the page that contains a merged CCO
        followed by ".CCO_" and then suffixed by the index of the page within the CCO.  The value
        of this variable is the name of the image file that was appended to the CCO. For example
        "TM000002.CCO_2" with a value of "TM000003.tif" indicates the primary page is TM000002
        and the second CCO that merged corresponds to image file "TM000003.tif". One of these entries
        is created for each page that is merged.<br/><br/>

        <b>mergedimagefile</b> : This variable is created based on the previous variable.  The
        value of the variable is the height of the merged image.
        For example, the value of the variable is "TM000003.TIF" the value could be 4800.  That means
        the height of the merged CCO was 4800 pixels. One entry is created for each page merged.<br/><br/>

        <e>
          <b>MergeCCOsByType("Invoice_Page,Invoice_Cont")</b><br/><br/>

          The <b>MergeCCOsByType</b> example above will merged all page types that are of "Invoice_Page"
          and "Invoice_Cont" into a single CCO.  The CCO for the first page that matches
          either of these types will become the primary CCO with all of the merged
          pages.  All of the pages under the current document will be merged together.
          If pages of the same type exist in the batch, but they are associated with a
          different document object, they will not be included in the merged CCO.<br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <see>
        Refer to the top-level help topic for more information about fingerprints and CCO files.<br/><br/>

        Refer to the top-level help topic for the "SharedRecognitionTools" action library
        for additional information regarding the CCO file.<br/><br/>

          The action AdjustMergedCCOPositions in the ZonesAndLineItems action library will
          adjust the positions in the merged CCO.<br/><br/>
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="MergeLayoutByType" access="public" qi="Merges the layout files (*layout.xml) associated with Page objects of the Document Hierarchy.">
      <p name="TypesToMerge" type="string" />
      <ap>
        Comma-separated string values indicating the Page Types of the Document Hierarchy objects to be merged.
        Smart parameters are supported.
      </ap>
      <lvl>Document level only.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if a layout file (*layout.xml) for one of the Page Types is not available or if the merged layout
        cannot be created. Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <h>
        Merges the layout files (layout.xml) associated with Page objects of the Document Hierarchy.
        The layout.xml for the first page merged will become the new merged layout.xml.
        The merged layout.xml can be then loaded to create a merged CCO for the merged pages.
        The layout file for the first merged page will be updated to contain
        the recognition results from the layout block information for all of the merged pages.<br/><br/>

        Only pages contained in the current document object will be merged. A layout file must
        exist for each of the pages to be merged. Only page types indicated by the parameter
        will be included in the merged file.<br/><br/>

        This action can only be attached to a document node so a document structure must exist.
        The document node's child pages will be merged if the page type matches one of the types
        provided as a parameter.
        <e>
          <b>MergeLayoutByType("Main_Page,Trailing_Page")</b><br/><br/>

          The <b>MergeLayoutByType</b> action above combines all of the text of the pages in the current DCO document
          of type Main_Page and Trailing_Page into
          a single, searchable layout file.
        </e>
      </h>
    </method>


    <method name="SetFilterClassName" access="public" qi="Sets the Fingerprint Class filter for the identification (matching) algorithm.">
      <p name="ClassName" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>ClassName:</b> The name Fingerprint Class you want to use as the filter. Smart Parameters are supported.<br/>
      </ap>
      <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <h>
        Sets the Fingerprint Class filter for the identification (matching) algorithm.
        The filter will force the fingerprint-matching algorithm to use only fingerprints
        associated with the specified Fingerprint Class.  This can improve accuracy and
        performance by limiting the search of fingerprints to smaller subset of fingerprints
        within the application.<br/><br/>

        To disable this filter, call with an empty string as the parameter. If this action is not called then
        this filter is not enabled.<br/><br/>

        Once set, this setting will be in effect for the length of the current task profile or until it is change.
        If fingerprint actions are used again in a later task profile, then this action must be called again
        to configure the setting before using subsequent fingerprint actions.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>SetFilterClassName("MyFingerprintClass")</b><br/>
          FindFingerprint("False","")<br/><br/>

          This example configures the fingerprint class prior to calling find fingerprint.  Only fingerprints
          associated with the specified class will be matched. This example shows the filter is set just prior to
          FindFingerprint for illustration.  As long as this action has been called by a prior rule, the configured
          filter will be in effect for the length of the current profile, or until SetFilterClassName is called
          again with a different parameter.<br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <see>
        Refer to the top-level action help topic for more information about fingerprints and this action library.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="SetFilterPageType" access="public" qi="Sets the Page Type filter for the identification (matching) algorithm.">
      <p name="PageType" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>PageType:</b> The Page Type you want to use as the filter.  Smart parameters are supported.<br/>
      </ap>
      <lvl>All levels, but generally at the Page level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <h>
        Sets the Page Type Filter For the identification (matching) algorithm.
        The filter will force the fingerprint-matching algorithm to use only fingerprints
        associated with the specified fingerprint type.  This can improve accuracy and
        performance by limiting the search of fingerprints to smaller subset of fingerprints
        within the application.<br/><br/>

        A single page type is allowed to have multiple fingerprints.  For example, a page type could
        be an invoice.  While an invoice will typically always have the same kind of data such as
        dates, invoice numbers, vendor, shipping information, line item information, etc., the location
        of the text on the page can be different on invoice from vendor "A" compared to the location
        of data on an invoice from vendor "B".  The fingerprint will define the location of the data
        based on the specific fingerprint that was matched.<br/><br/>

        To disable this filter, call with an empty string as the parameter. If this action is not called then
        this filter is not enabled.<br/><br/>

        Once set, this setting will be in effect for the length of the current task profile, unless it is changed.
        If fingerprint actions are used again in a later task profile, then this action must be called again
        to configure the setting before using subsequent fingerprint actions.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>SetFilterPageType("Invoice_Page")</b><br/>
          FindFingerprint("False","")<br/><br/>

          This example configures the fingerprint class prior to calling find fingerprint.  Only fingerprints
          associated with the specified class will be matched. This example shows the filter is set just prior to
          FindFingerprint for illustration.  As long as this action has been called by a prior rule, the configured
          filter will be in effect for the length of the current profile, or until SetFilterPageType is called
          again with a different parameter.<br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <see>
        Refer to the top-level action help topic for more information about fingerprints and this action library.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="SetFingerprint" access="public" qi="Sets a newly created fingerprint's Fingerprint Class and Fingerprint Class ID values. ">
      <p name="FingerprintClass" type="string" />
      <p name="FingerprintID" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>FingerprintClass:</b> The Fingerprint Class value.<br/>

        <b>FingerprintID:</b> (optional) The Fingerprint ID value. All fingerprints must have a unique ID.
        If left blank, a unique ID will be assigned automatically. It is recommended to allow the
        fingerprint ID to be assigned automatically.<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters. <br/>
      </ap>
      <lvl>Page level only.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if either parameter is invalid.  Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <h>
        After using the action AddFingerprint to add a new fingerprint to the set of
        fingerprints for the application, use this action to set the newly created fingerprint's Fingerprint Class
        and Fingerprint Class ID values.<br/><br/>

        The fingerprint class and ID are added to the Host table of the
        application's Fingerprint database - as RefName and RefID values.<br/><br/>
          
          The combination of the actions AddFingerprint() followed by SetFingerprint() may only be used
          when the fingerprint service is not enabled.  If the fingerprint service is enabled,
          and a fingerprint host class ID must also be set for the fingerprint,
          then you must use the AddAndSetFingerprint() action instead of the combination of 
          AddFingerprint and SetFingerprint().<br/><br/>
        <e>
          AddFingerprint()<br/>
          <b>SetFingerprint("@P\VendorName","@P\VendorID")</b><br/><br/>

            In this example, smart parameter notation obtains the values at runtime from the field text of the
            fields VendorName and VendorID and the text values of those fields
            will populate the Host table of the application's Fingerprint Database.
            Use this action combination only when the fingerprint
            service is not enabled. If the fingerprint service is enabled, use AddAndSetFingerprint.<br/>
        </e>
      </h>
        <see>
            AddAndSetFingerprint
        </see>
    </method>


    <method name="SetFingerprintDirectory" access="public" qi="Sets the Fingerprint directory of your application.">
      <p name="Directory" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>Directory:</b> the path to the directory where the fingerprints (.cco files) are stored for the application. Smart parameters are supported.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <lvl>All levels.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Returns <b>True,</b> if successful.  Otherwise, <b>False</b> and sets the batch to an abort state.
      </ret>
      <h>
        Sets the Fingerprint directory of your application. This directory contains the application's fingerprints.
        This action must be called prior to creating or finding fingerprints.<br/><br/>

        This action must be called if using local fingerprints or if the fingerprint service is used.
        When using the application service, call SetFingerprintWebServiceURL prior to this action.<br/><br/>

        This action only needs to be called once within a task profile.  It can be called at any level but is generally
        called on a batch level object on a batch open event.  If this is placed on a lower level object, it may then
        be called multiple times, which is unnecessary.  The setting is in effect for the length of the task profile.
        If fingerprint actions are used again in a later task profile, then this action must be called again
        to configure the fingerprint directory before using subsequent fingerprint actions.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>SetFingerprintDirectory("C:\ParentDirectory\Application\Fingerprint")</b><br/><br/>

          The action above identifies the location and path of an application's Fingerprint directory.<br/><br/><br/>

          <b>SetFingerprintWebServiceURL(@APPVAR(FPServiceURL)</b><br/>
          SetFingerprintDirectory(@APPPATH(fingerprint))<br/><br/>

          This example is similar to the previous example. The difference is that the values of the parameters
          are obtained from the application manager settings using smart parameter notation.  Storing values in the application manager allows
          a single location to place application settings.  Commonly settings that could be different between
          production and test systems are stored here.  This allows an application to be deployed without any
          changes to the rules it would simply be the application manager configuration settings that
          contain the correct values for the test environment or the production environment.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <see>
        Refer to the top-level help topic of this action library for more information about fingerprints.
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="SetFingerprintSearchArea" access="public" qi="Controls the portion of the current page that will be used to find a matching fingerprint.">
      <p name="MatchStart"/>
      <p name="MatchEnd"/>
      <ap>
        <b>MatchStart:</b> A decimal value from 0.01 (1%) to 1.00 (all) to indicate how much of the page is to be matched.
        If the second parameter is empty the first parameter represents the bottom of the area for matching, starting from the top of the page.
        If the second parameter is not empty, the first parameter is the top or start of the area for matching.
        For example, a single parameter of 0.5 indicates that fingerprint matching will be limited to the first half of the page (0 to 50%).
        Decimal separators must be appropriate for the current locale.
        In applications which might be used in locales with different decimal separators, use percentage notation.
        <br/><br/>

        <b>MatchEnd:</b> Optional: A decimal value from 0.01 (1%) to 1.0 (all) to indicate the end point on the page to be
        used for fingerprint matching.  If this parameter is supplied, the fist parameter is the starting point.
        For example: if the first parameter is 0.6, and the second parameter is 1.0,
        the last 40% of the page will be used for fingerprint matching (60-100%).<br/><br/>

        Both parameters allow either a decimal value or a percentage.  The decimal values must match the decimal format for the current locale.
        To indicate the value is a "Percentage" the action can use a number followed by "p" - 50p, for example - to represent 50%.<br/><br/>

        When using the percentage values, the number must be a whole number, and cannot contain a decimal separator.<br/>
        Decimal separators must be appropriate for the current locale.
        In applications which might be used in locales with different decimal separators, use percentage notation.<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.
      </ap>
      <h>
        This action uses the numeric value(s) you supply to determine the portion of the current page
        that will be used to find a matching fingerprint.<br/><br/>

        This setting will be in effect for the length of the task profile that is running the rules.
        If fingerprint actions are used again in a later task profile, then this action must be called again
        to configure the search area before using subsequent fingerprint actions.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>SetFingerprintSearchArea("0.5","")</b><br/><br/>

          The example above compares lines and words in the upper 50% of the current page to the lines and
          words in the same portion of each fingerprint.  Notice the second parameter has been left blank.<br/><br/>

          <b>SetFingerprintSearchArea("0.5","1.0")</b><br/><br/>

          This example compares lines and words in the lower 50% of the current page to the lines and
          words in the same portion of each fingerprint. Remember: you can replace a parameter's decimal
          value with a percentage(p) or metric(m) number.
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All levels, but generally at the Page level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the first parameter is missing or is not numeric. Also returns <b>False</b>
        if the second parameter is not numeric.  Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top-level help topic for more information regarding fingerprints and this action library.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="SetFingerprintWebServiceApplicationID" access="public" qi="Use this action to specify unique application ID.">
      <p name="ApplicationID"/>
      <ap>
        <b>ApplicationID:</b> A unique Application ID.  This value used to retrieve correct list of fingerprints loaded to the server.<br/>
        Smart parameters are supported.<br/>
      </ap>
      <lvl>Batch, Document, or Page levels.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if action SetFingerprintWebServiceURL() has not been called.  Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <h>
        By specifying an application ID, it will cause the fingerprint service to segregate the fingerprints
        from this calling application from other applications.  This will prevent fingerprints from another
        application being matched with images from this application.  It will also limit the fingerprint
        matching to the fingerprints within this application only.<br/><br/>

        Unless there is a specific need to match fingerprints across different applications, then
        each application that uses the fingerprint service should use its own unique application ID
        to separate its fingerprints and fingerprint matching from other applications.<br/><br/>

        Once this action is called, it remains in effect for the life of the current task profile.
        It is typically called on a batch open event and does not need to be called over and
        over on each page when FindFingerprint or other fingerprint actions are called.
        If fingerprint actions are used again in a later task profile, then this action must be called again
        to configure the application ID before using subsequent fingerprint actions.<br/><br/>

        If this action is not called, then the default application name "datacap.ccodb" will
        be used. To configure the use of an application name, this action must be called
        immediately before or immediately after SetFingerprintWebServiceURL.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          SetFingerprintWebServiceURL("http://'FPSERVERNAME'/fpservice/Service.asmx?WSDL")<br/>
          <b>SetFingerprintWebServiceApplicationID("1040ez")</b><br/><br/>

          This example enables the application service by setting the URL, causing subsequent actions
          to be handled by the fingerprint service.  The action SetFingerprintWebServiceApplicationID
          configures all of the subsequent operations from this application to be processed using
          the application ID "1040ez".<br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <see>
        Refer to the top-level help topic for more information regarding fingerprints and the fingerprint service.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="SetFingerprintWebServiceFailureThreshold" access="public" qi="Uses this action to specify percentage of fingerprint upload failures to ignore.">
      <p name="IgnorePercent"/>
      <ap>
        <b>IgnorePercent:</b> An integer that represents percent threshold of fingerprint upload failures to ignore.
        The batch will abort if the percentage of fingerprints that are failed to load exceeds this value.<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported.
      </ap>
      <lvl>Batch, Document, or Page levels.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Returns <b>False</b> when:<br/>
        The fingerprint service is not configured using action SetFingerprintWebServiceURL().<br/>

        Returns <b>False</b> and aborts the batch when:<br/>
        The input parameter is not an integer value from 0 to 100.<br/>
        If the percentage of fingerprints that failed to load is greater than the threshold.<br/>
        If no fingerprints have been loaded and at least one has failed to load.<br/><br/>

        Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <h>
        Uses this action to set the maximum number of fingerprint upload failures to ignore.
        If the application has been set using the action SetFingerprintWebServiceApplicationID, then the threshold
        will be set for that specific application only.<br/><br/>

        This action is only used with the fingerprint service and SetFingerprintWebServiceURL must
        be called prior to this action.<br/><br/>

        If some fingerprints are not able to be loaded, that may or may not be a problem, depending
        on the application.  This action will configure the threshold that will determine if too many missing
        fingerprints needs to be considered an error.  Once this threshold is reached, it will likely cause
        all subsequent batches to fail as the fingerprint service will be considered to be non-operational
        until the fingerprint issue is resolved.<br/><br/>

        The fingerprint service will load fingerprints only when an action that requires searching
        of fingerprints is called, such as FindFingerprint or FindBlackFingerprint.
        If the fingerprint service has not previously been called by an earlier batch using  the
        same application ID, or if the current batch has not yet called an action that loads
        the fingerprints for the specified application, then the service will report that no
        fingerprints have been loaded and this action will still return true.<br/><br/>

        This action typically only needs to be called once per batch.  Calling it multiple times
        is allowed but is unnecessary and will take some amount of processing time.  Configure your rules
        so this action is called once after the fingerprints are loaded and not so it is called
        over and over within a single batch.  The setting will exist for the length of the
        current task profile that is running rules.
        If fingerprint actions are used again in a later task profile, then this action must be called again
        to configure this setting again before using subsequent fingerprint actions.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>SetFingerprintWebServiceURL("http://127.0.0.1/fpservice/Service.asmx?WSDL")</b><br/>
          SetFingerprintDirectory(@APPPATH(fingerprint))<br/>
          FindFingerprint("False","")<br/>
          <b>SetFingerprintFailureThreshold("10")</b><br/><br/>

            This example enables the use of the fingerprint service by configuring the URL for the service,
            configures the fingerprint directory for the application, finds the fingerprint
            for the current page and then checks to see if the number of failed fingerprints
            exceeds the allowed percentage.  If it does exceed the maximum, the action returns
            false and the batch will be set to abort.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
    </method>


    <method name="SetFingerprintWebServiceURL" access="public" qi="Provides the Uniform Resource Locator (URL) that is a link to a pre-established Fingerprint Service.">
      <p name="URL"/>
      <ap>
        <b>URL:</b> The URL that is the link to the Fingerprint Web Service. Smart parameters are supported.
      </ap>
      <lvl>All levels.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <h>
        This action configures the URL for the fingerprint service.  If using the fingerprint service, this action must
        be called prior to an action that communicates with the service, such as FindFingerprint and FindBlackFingerprint.
        Once connected, then the fingerprint service will be used for all subsequent calls.  The action SetFingerprintDirectory
        must also be configured prior to using the fingerprint service.  When using the fingerprint service,
        SetFingerprintWebService should be called within the task profile from this action library.<br/><br/>

        This action will use fingerprint web service if fingerprint web URL is set by SetFingerprintWebServiceURL and if the
        service is available. If the fingerprint web service is not available, then it will default to local fingerprint service.<br/><br/>>

        This action should not be called if the application is using "local" fingerprints only.  The top-level help topic
        for this action provides more information about the fingerprint service and the situations where the fingerprint
        service should be used instead of local fingerprints.  The fingerprint service must be configured and running
        prior to running an application that uses the service.<br/><br/>

        This action only needs to be called once within a task profile.  It can be called at any level but is generally
        called on a batch level object on a batch open event.  If this is placed on a lower level object, it may then
        be called multiple times, which is unnecessary.  The setting is in effect for the length of the task profile.
        If fingerprint actions are used again in a later task profile, then this action must be called again
        to configure the fingerprint service URL before using subsequent fingerprint actions.<br/><br/>
        <br/>
        <br/>
        <e>
          <b>SetFingerprintWebServiceApplicationID("MyApplication")</b><br/>
          <b>SetFingerprintWebServiceURL("http://'FPSERVERNAME'/fpservice/Service.asmx?WSDL")</b><br/>
          SetFingerprintDirectory("\ParentDirectory\Application\Fingerprint")<br/><br/>

          This example sets the application name for the fingerprint service and then configures
          the web service URL and also sets the fingerprint directory. This example
          would typically be called on a batch level object.<br/><br/><br/>


          <b>SetFingerprintWebServiceURL(@APPVAR(FPServiceURL)</b><br/>
          SetFingerprintDirectory(@APPPATH(fingerprint))<br/><br/>

          This example is similar to the previous example. The difference is that the values of the parameters
          are obtained from the application manager settings using smart parameter notation.  Storing values in the application manager allows
          a single location to place application settings.  Commonly settings that could be different between
          production and test systems are stored here.  This allows an application to be deployed without any
          changes to the rules it would simply be the application manager configuration settings that
          contain the correct values for the test environment or the production environment.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <see>
        Refer to the top-level help topic of this action library for more information about fingerprints and the fingerprint service.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="SetMaximumMatchingOffset" access="public" qi="Sets the Maximum Offset value while matching source pages to fingerprints.">
      <p name="MaximumOffset"/>
      <ap>
        <b>MaximumOffset:</b> Integer value between 1 and 255. Smart parameters are supported.
      </ap>
      <lvl>All levels.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the parameter is invalid.  Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <h>
        Sets the Maximum Offset value while matching source pages to fingerprints.
        The actual shift is four times the maximum offset value in pixels (4 * MaxOffset).
        Increasing this value improves but slows the matching process.<br/><br/>

        If this action is not called, the default value is 6: 4 * 6 = 24 pixels.<br/><br/>

        This action has no effect when used with the Fingerprint Service<br/><br/>

        Once set, this setting will be in effect for the length of the current task profile.
        If fingerprint actions are used again in a later task profile, then this action must be called again
        to configure the setting before using subsequent fingerprint actions.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>SetMaximumMatchingOffset("12")</b><br/><br/>

          This example results in a Maximum Offset value of 48 pixels, well above the 24 pixel default.
        </e>
      </h>
      <see>
        Refer to the top-level help topic for more information about fingerprints.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="SetMinimumMatchingTolerance" access="public" qi="Uses the decimal value you supply as a parameter to set a minimum Matching Tolerance Rating.">
      <p name="Minimum" qi="The minimum matching tolerance level."/>
      <ap>
        <b>Minimum:</b> A decimal value from 0.00 (Lowest Tolerance) to 0.99 (Highest Tolerance).
        The decimal separator must be appropriate for the locale. Smart parameters are supported.<br/>
      </ap>
      <lvl>All, but generally at the Page level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the parameter is missing or the parameter is not numeric. Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <h>
        This action sets the minimum confidence matching tolerance rating to determine if a fingerprint is a match.
        If none of the existing fingerprints match with the specified tolerance or greater, then it is
        considered that none of the existing fingerprints correctly match the
        fingerprint for the current page.<br/><br/>

        A non-matching fingerprint is not necessarily an error.  For some applications, all of the expected types
        of pages are known. Maybe there are 10 known page types or perhaps there are 10,000 known page types, but they
        are all known when the application goes into production so it is expected that the FindFingerprint action
        should match one of the predetermined fingerprints.  If it does not match, then the page may have been
        ingested as an error, or perhaps needs other handling to be correctly processed.<br/><br/>

        For other applications, such as APT, they may be deployed with a set of known fingerprints, but can also
        learn new fingerprints as the application is used.  For example, a new vendor starts sending a new kind
        of document. Fingerprint matching would also fail in that case, however, for APT, it would cause
        alternate processing which will define the fingerprint at runtime during the course of processing the
        new document.  Once the document has been fully processed, a new fingerprint will be generated, allowing
        a successful fingerprint match the next time that new page format is ingested.<br/><br/>

        A lower tolerance rating will allow greater chance for a match of a fingerprint, but also a greater chance for a <b>False</b> match.
        It is recommended to adjust the tolerance against the set of fingerprints for an application to allow for poor quality documents
        to successfully match the fingerprint, but not so low that fingerprints match incorrectly.<br/><br/>

        Once set, this setting will be in effect for the length of the current task profile.
        If fingerprint actions are used again in a later task profile, then this action must be called again
        to configure the setting before using subsequent fingerprint actions.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          AnalyzeImage()<br/>
          RotateImage()<br/>
          RecognizePageOCR_S()<br/>
          SetSearchArea("0.0","0.5")<br/>
          <b>SetMinimumMatchingTolerance("0.70")</b><br/>
          FindFingerprint("True","")<br/><br/>

          In this sequence, the fingerprint is created for the current page to create the CCO and the recognition results are also
          placed into the CCO.  The search area is configured to the top 50% of the page and the matching tolerance is set to ".7".
          The FindFingerprint action then attempts to match the fingerprint for the current page to the existing set
          of fingerprints.  If a fingerprint does not match the minimum tolerance, then the fingerprint for the
          new page is added to the set of existing fingerprints.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <see>
        Refer to the top-level help topic for more information about fingerprints.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="SetPageFingerprintID" access="public" bInter="bInter" bDebug="bDebug" strParam="StrParam" qi="Assigns a value to the FingerprintID property of the selected Page object of the Document Hierarchy.">
      <p name="FingerprintID" qi="The fingerprint ID to associate with the page."/>
      <ap>
        <b>FingerprintID:</b> The fingerprint ID, sometimes called the template ID, to assign to this page.<br/>
        Smart parameters are supported.
      </ap>
      <h>
        Assigns a value to the FingerprintID property of the selected Page object of the Document Hierarchy.
        This links a specific fingerprint template ID with the page.  The fingerprint template identifies the
        zone locations for each field within the page, associating an area of the image to
        the location of the text represented by the field.<br/><br/>

        Typically fingerprint templates are automatically assigned with the action FindFingerprint which will
        automatically identify the fingerprint template that matches the document.  However,
        there are times when alternate techniques can be used to match a page to a specific fingerprint
        template.  This action assigns a specific fingerprint template ID to the page.<br/><br/>

        A common use for this action is to first determine what it is about that page that
        determines which fingerprint should be associated with the page. One typical mechanism is
        determining the fingerprint by some unique text on the page. Other methods are possible
        such as the name of the source file or other attributes that can be detected about the page.<br/><br/>

        Once the use case is understood, then rules can be written to determine which fingerprint
        template ID should be assigned.  Once determined by the rules, then this action can be
        used to assign a specific fingerprint template ID to the page.  Once assigned, then
        the zones can be loaded to assign the position locations to the page's fields.<br/><br/>

        The <b>SetPageFingerprintID</b> action
        will create a fingerprint ID property/variable "TemplateID" of the
        current Page object if it does not already exist.<br/><br/>

        This action is functionally equivalent to the following generic action:<br/>
        rrSet("1010","@X.TemplateID")<br/>
        However, using SetPageFingerprintID can add additional context to the intention
        of setting the fingerprint.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          RecognizePageOCR_A()<br/>
          WordFind("MQSW")<br/>
          <b>SetPageFingerprintID("1010")</b><br/><br/>

          In this sequence, the page is recognized then the recognized text
          is searched to see if it contains "MQSQ", which is an example of some
          kind of unique text that identifies the page.
          If the WordFind action locates "MQSW" on the current page,
          the SetPageFingerprintID action assigns "1010" as the page's
          Fingerprint ID. This links the page to a fingerprint with a Fingerprint ID of "1010"
          as was setup in the Zones tab of Datacap Studio.<br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Page level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if the rule is applied at the Page level.  Otherwise, <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        FindFingerprint, ReadZones, ReadZonesFPX<br/>
        Refer to the top-level help topic for more information about fingerprints.
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="UpdateFingerprintStatistics" access="public" qi="Updates the Fingerprint hit statistics.">
      <ap>
        None.
      </ap>
      <lvl>Page level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if called from any level other than Page level, or the Fingerprint database is not accessible.  Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <h>
        Fingerprint information is stored in the Fingerprint Database.  One piece of information is a count
        of the number of times a fingerprint is matched.  This information can be useful for in
        some instances. Use this action to increment the count for the current page and update the fingerprint statistics
        in the fingerprint database.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          FindFingerprint("False","")<br/>
          <b>UpdateFingerprintStatistics()</b><br/><br/>

          This example calls UpdateFingerprintStatistics after matching the fingerprint to increment the count for the
          number of times the specific fingerprint associated with the current page is matched.<br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <see>
        Refer to the top-level help topic for more information about fingerprints.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>


      <method name="XMLReadZones" qi="Loads position information for current fingerprint.">
          <ap>None.</ap>
          <h>
              This action reads the field zone information from the fingerprint XML file associated with the fingerprint for
              the current page object and sets the zone coordinates for the fields from the zoned fingerprint.               
              This action is similar to the ReadZones action in the ZonesAndLineItems library.
              The primary difference is that ReadZones reads the zone information stored in the setup DCO from legacy
              fingerprints and XMLReadZones
              reads the zone information from the associated fingerprint XML file.
              Typically this action will be called after the CreateFields action but before field recognition.<br/><br/>

              XMLReadZones loads position information for each field in the calling object and it's children. If available, it adjusts
              these values based on offset information stored in the 'Image_Offset' variable at any node level.
              The offset value is applied to all child objects of a node where an 'Image_Offset' variable is found;
              unless overwritten by a child node also having an Image_Offset value to apply.<br/><br/>

              Position information is based on the Fingerprint XML position for the parent page's fingerprint ID.<br/><br/>

              If 'Enable FPXML' setting is checked in the application settings 'Main' tab within the Taskmaster Application Manager,
              when zoning fingerprints Datacap Studio will place field zone information into a fingerprint XML file instead of placing the zone
              information into the applications setup DCO.  One XML file is created per fingerprint.
              Depending on application needs, using fingerprint XML files may have
              advantages over legacy fingerprints.  
              If the application is using fingerprint XML files, then use this action to load the zones instead of the ReadZones action.
              <br/><br/>
              <e>
                  SetFingerprintDirectory("@APPPATH(*/fingerprint)") <br/>
                  <b>XMLReadZones()</b>
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>Page or Field level.</lvl>
          <ret>
              <b>False,</b> if the fingerprint xml cannot be loaded or if SetFingerprintDirectory has not
              been called to set the location of the fingerprint XML files. Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
          </ret>
          <see>
              The top-level help of this action library contains an explanation of XML fingerprints.
          </see>
      </method>

      <method name="XMLSetDetailsAndLineitemPair" qi="Sets the type of the special Details and Lineitem fields.">
          <p name="DetailFieldType" qi="The type for the current page's detail field, if one exists."/>
          <p name="LineitemFieldType" qi="The type for the current page's line item field, if one exists."/>
          <ap>
              Two comma separated parameters:<br/><br/>

              <b>Detail field type:</b> type of the field that is the parent to the line item field, often called "Details". <br/><br/>
              <b>LineItem field type:</b> type of the line item field which is the parent of the fields to be captured, often called "Lineitem".<br/><br/>

              Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.
          </ap>
          <h>
              Use this action if your page contains "Details" and "Lineitems" fields.
              The action sets the type of the special Details and Lineitem fields. These fields, unlike other field types,
              are handled by the XMLReadZones and XMLWriteZones actions in a special way that is compatible
              with the Locate actions framework.<br/><br/>

              If your page has Details and Lineitems, then this action is required to identify the types
              so the position information will be properly saved.
              Using SetDetailsAndLineitemPairFPX prior to calling XMLWriteZones will cause XMLWriteZones to save line
              item field positions to the fingerprint file. The second parameter is the type of the line item
              field which is the parent of the fields to be captured and is often called "Lineitem".<br/><br/>

              If your page contains Detail and Lineitem fields, this action also needs to be used prior
              to calling XMLReadZones.<br/><br/>

              Details are the parent object of the line item fields.  Details are a collection of lineitems
              and each line item has a collection of fields.  For example, in a typical invoice
              application a single lineitem can contain the fields: quantity, item number, description, unit price, and total.<br/><br/>
              
              This setting will stay in effect until the current task profile completes.<br/><br/>
              <e>
                  <b>XMLSetDetailsAndLineitemPair("Details","Lineitem")</b><br/>
                  XMLReadZones()<br/><br/>

                  In this example, the details field is called "Details" and the line items are called "Lineitem".<br/><br/><br/>

                  An example DCO hierarchy may look something like this:<br/>
                  - Details<br/>
                  --- Lineitem<br/>
                  ------- Qty<br/>
                  ------- ItemID<br/>
                  ------- ItemDesc<br/>
                  ------- Price<br/>
                  ------- LineTotal<br/>
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
          <ret>
              <b>True.</b>
          </ret>
          <see>
              The top-level help of this action library contains an explanation of XML fingerprints.
          </see>
      </method>

      <method name="XMLWriteZones"  qi="Writes position information for all fields of the page.">
          <p name="FingerprintHostName" qi="Target fingerprint class name."/>
          <p name="FingerprintHostID" qi="Target fingerprint host ID."/>
          <p name="FingerprintPageType" qi="Target fingerprint page type."/>
          <ap>
              <b>Fingerprint host name:</b> This is the fingerprint class name and can be seen in the zones tab of Datacap Studio.
              It is used to group or classify fingerprints.
              If this value is not provided, then a blank class name will be used.<br/><br/>

              <b>Fingerprint host id:</b> This is an internal numeric value that is associated with the fingerprint class name.
              If a value is not provided, a blank value will be used.<br/><br/>

              <b>Fingerprint page type:</b> This is the page type that corresponds to the fingerprint for the page.
              If this value is not provided, it uses the page type of the current page.<br/><br/>

              Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.  All parameters are optional.<br/>  
          </ap>
          <h>
              This action will write out all of the field positions for the current page to the fingerprint XML file.
              Typically this action is used for applications that are using fingerprint XML files and new pages
              are dynamically zoned at runtime.  After the zoning of the page has been completed, then this action
              is used to write out the zone information to the FPXML file, allowing subsequent pages of this
              type to be processed using the saved zone information.  If pages are not being zoned by a verify operator
              at runtime, then it is not necessary to use this action.<br/><br/>

              If the fingerprint directory that had been previously specified using SetFingerprintDirectory does not exist, it will be created,
              providing that the parent directory already exists.<br/><br/>

              Fields with empty positions are not written to the fingerprint XML file.<br/><br/>
              <e>
                  SetFingerprintDirectory("@APPPATH(*/fingerprint)")<br/>
                  <b>XMLWriteZones("MyFPClass,,MyPageType")</b><br/><br/>

                  This example writes out the zones for the current page and sets the fingerprint class to "MyFPClass" and
                  the page type to "MyPageType".<br/><br/>

                  SetFingerprintDirectory("@APPPATH(*/fingerprint)")<br/>
                  <b>XMLWriteZones("MyFPClass,,@P.MyType")</b><br/><br/>

                  This example writes out the zones for the current page and sets the fingerprint class to "MyFPClass" and
                  the page type to the value contained by the page variable "MyType".<br/><br/>
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>Page level only.</lvl>
          <ret>
              <b>False,</b> if there is a problem saving to fingerprint XML file or if not called at the page level,
              if the page does not have a fingerprint assigned, or a fingerprint directory has not been set. Otherwise <b>True.</b>
          </ret>
          <see>
              The top-level help of this action library contains an explanation of XML fingerprints.
          </see>
      </method>

  </com>
</rrx>
